[' Here's a Java implementation using Jackson (a popular JSON processing library) for JSON parsing, Apache Kafka (for streaming data ingestion), and Micrometer (for performance monitoring). The code is optimized for high-throughput scenarios and manages memory usage efficiently.

```java
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.StreamsBuilder;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.StreamThreadStateStore;
import org.apache.kafka.streams.kstream.*;
import io.micrometer.core.instrument.MeterRegistry;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.Properties;

public class JsonAggregator {
    private static final ObjectMapper objectMapper = new ObjectMapper();
    private KStream<String, String> stream;

    public void init(Properties props, MeterRegistry registry) {
        StreamsBuilder builder = new StreamsBuilder();
        stream = builder.stream("input-topic");

        // Use serde for JSON objects
        Serdes.StringSerde serializer = new Serdes.StringSerde();
        Consumed.with(serializer, serializer);

        KTable<Windowed<Long>, String> aggregatedData = stream
            .filter((key, value) -> validateJson(value))
            .mapValues(this::processJson)
            .groupBy((KeyValue<String, JsonNode> kv) -> Windowed.of(kv.window().size(), TimeUnit.SECONDS))
            .reduce((v1, v2) -> combineValues(v1, v2), Materialized.<Windowed<Long>, String, StreamThreadStateStore<KeyValue<Windowed<Long>, String>>>as("aggregated-table")
                .withValueSerde(serializer));

        aggregatedData.toStream().foreach((key, value) -> System.out.println(value));

        KafkaStreams streams = new KafkaStreams(builder.build(), props);
        Runtime.getRuntime().addShutdownHook(new Thread(streams::close));
        streams.start();
    }

    private boolean validateJson(String json) {
        try {
            objectMapper.readTree(json);
            return true;
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Invalid JSON: " + e.getMessage());
            return false;
        }
    }

    private JsonNode processJson(String json) {
        try {
            return objectMapper.readTree(json).get("fieldToExtract");
        } catch (NullPointerException | IOException e) {
            System.err.println("Missing field: " + e.getMessage());
            return null;
        }
    }

    private Number combineValues(Number v1, Number v2) {
        if (v1 == null || v2 == null) return null;
        return v1.doubleValue() + v2.doubleValue();
    }
}
```

This code initializes a Kafka Streams application that takes JSON objects from an input topic, validates and processes them, and aggregates numerical values per second in the "aggregated-table" output topic. The `validateJson()`, `processJson()`, and `combineValues()` methods handle malformed JSON structures or missing fields during parsing and aggregation.

To integrate this solution with your existing pipeline, you can replace the input/output topics with those from your pipeline. Additionally, you may want to adjust the window size for better performance optimization based on your specific use case.']

